    "q-184005": {
      "dom_id": "step-q184005",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\nIf $n$ is an even integer, then $3n+2$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an {{field-1}} integer. Then, $n=$ {{field-2}} for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting {{field-3}} into the expression $3n+2$, we can show that $3n+2=$ {{field-4}}, where $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, since $3n+2$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $3n+2$ is {{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,3n+2\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{even}$ integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $3n+2$ and show it is *even* by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+2$, we can show that $3n+2=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since $3n+2$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $3n+2$ is $\\text{even}.$",
